<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is the difference between cellulitis and a facial abscess?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Cellulitis is a diffuse spreading skin infection without a defined boundary — the skin is red and inflamed throughout. An abscess is a walled-off collection of pus with a defined lump. Abscesses require drainage; cellulitis typically responds to antibiotics alone.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Will I need surgery for a facial infection?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Most facial infections are treated with antibiotics alone. Surgery is needed when there is a collection of pus (abscess) that requires drainage, or when necrotic tissue needs removal. Your surgeon will determine this after examination.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How do I prevent facial infection recurrence?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Good skin hygiene, prompt cleaning of cuts or abrasions, avoiding squeezing cysts or pimples, and managing conditions like acne or eczema reduce recurrence risk. Any persistent or recurring facial lesion should be evaluated professionally.</div>
</details>
</div>
